chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 13 of the 3rd edition teaches how a program reaches the web in five steps of growing power: the webbrowser module that opens a page in the user's own browser, the requests module that downloads a page or a file, an API of a weather service answered in JSON, the parsing of HTML with Beautiful Soup, and the control of a real browser with Selenium and with Playwright; it builds three projects on them, showmap.py for OpenStreetMap, searchpypi.py for the search results of PyPI and downloadXkcdComics.py for a web comic, and closes with 18 practice questions and three practice programs, an image site downloader, the game 2048 and link verification." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's printed behaviours reproduce wherever they can run offline: the map URL of showmap.py with its address quoted, the status codes and the message of a failed download, the byte counts that write() returns while a response is saved in chunks, the conversion of 285.44 Kelvin to 12.3 Celsius and 54.1 Fahrenheit, and, with requests 2.34.2 and Beautiful Soup 4.15.0 run on the page that the chapter downloads, the matches, texts and attributes the chapter prints for the span with the id author and for the first two paragraphs; the page as downloaded and the book's own ontology agree byte for byte, since the SHA-256 of the chapter as fetched equals the digest the ontology records, and the file example3.html of 957 bytes has the SHA-256 c7a82b39 and Windows line ends." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P08, chx:P15, chx:P17, chx:P40 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Ten printed things in the chapter cannot be run as printed: the HTML of example3.html as typeset has a typographic quotation mark inside the href of the link, an unescaped p tag in the first paragraph and a misplaced closing form tag, so that on it Beautiful Soup 4.15.0 finds no element with the id author and eight paragraphs, whereas the file the chapter tells the reader to download has the escaped text and the right tags; the chapter says that select('p') gives a list of three matches, but the file has seven paragraphs, three of them inside the form; the file's alt text reads Close up where the chapter prints Close-up, and its title is Example Website Title where the chapter says that the Playwright program prints Example Website (the page's h1 is Example Website, and both Selenium and Playwright printed the title in real Firefox); the innerHTML that the chapter prints shows <p> where the browser returns &lt;p&gt;; the Step 4 listing of the comic downloader is indented with 8, 12, 15, 12 and then 6 spaces and Python rejects it with an IndentationError; the printed list of sys.argv shows the words St, and Francisco, with a trailing space that shell splitting never produces; the printed error of raise_for_status names a URL that ends in .html although the requested URL does not; the length of the Romeo and Juliet file printed as 178978 is 174126 today; and the command python3 -m playwright install is typeset with an en dash, which Python reads as a file name." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P40, chx:P12, chx:P15, chx:P17, chx:P23 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Two of the chapter's three projects meet today's web differently from the book, and one of them depends on the Python release: fetching the search page of PyPI with requests on 2026-10-12 returned status 200 with the page titled Client Challenge and no element of the class package-snippet, so searchpypi.py opens nothing; the robots.txt of pypi.org disallows /search*, a rule that RFC 9309 defines with the wildcard *, and urllib.robotparser matched it as a literal prefix and answered True for can_fetch up to Python 3.14.4, but from Python 3.14.5 (released 2026-05-10, gh-138907, support for RFC 9309) it answers False, so that the same check allowed the program on the five releases 3.14.0 to 3.14.4 and forbids it on 3.14.5 and 3.14.6; the XKCD page structure the chapter relies on still held on the same day, with the comic image inside the element with id comic at an address beginning with two slashes, a rel prev link and # as the href of the prev link of the first comic." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P28, chx:P05, chx:P14, chx:P38, chx:P15 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's advice on requests is correct and incomplete: raise_for_status() after every get() is what the library documents, but the documentation also says that nearly all production code should pass a timeout because a call without one can hang indefinitely (a call with timeout=0.2 raised ReadTimeout, and the same call without one returned the slow server's late answer), and that a download to a file should pass stream=True, because without it the whole body is read before get() returns (with stream=True the first five bytes were read while the server still held the rest), so that iter_content(100000) on a response fetched without stream=True only cuts an already loaded body into chunks." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P15, chx:P16 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Current Python replaces or fences several details the chapter uses: PurePath.as_uri() is deprecated since Python 3.14 and removed in 3.19 in favour of Path.as_uri(), which gives the file:// URLs that the chapter suggests for local help pages; datetime.utcfromtimestamp() has been deprecated since 3.12, and datetime.fromtimestamp(), which the chapter names for the dt field of the forecast, answers in the local time zone of the machine, so the same timestamp printed 17:13 with the zone set to Europe/Istanbul and 07:13 with America/Los_Angeles; webbrowser.open() returns True or False and in 3.14 the BROWSER variable can name an already registered browser; and urllib.request.pathname2url has gained add_scheme in 3.14." ; sen0414:cites chx:P10, chx:P11, chx:P02, chx:P13, chx:P04, chx:P41 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The Selenium part of the chapter was executed in a real Firefox 157 with Selenium 4.51.0 and is one step behind the library: Selenium Manager, shipped with every Selenium release, downloaded and found the driver and the browser by itself, so the manual geckodriver download and the webdriver-manager package that the chapter mentions were not needed; the exception the chapter calls NoSuchElement is NoSuchElementException; the text of a WebElement comes from the WebDriver command GET_ELEMENT_TEXT and is not the innerText property that the chapter calls its source (for an element hidden with display none the text was an empty string while the innerText property was secret, and for an element styled with text-transform the text and the innerText were capitalised while textContent was not); get_attribute looks for a property first and then for an attribute; the chapter's claim that a Selenium-driven browser has the user agent of a regular browser held (Firefox/157.0), but the browser also reported navigator.webdriver as True, a flag a page can read; and find_element_by_id of older tutorials no longer exists in Selenium 4.51.0." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P19, chx:P18, chx:P20, chx:P21, chx:P34 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The Playwright part was executed in a real Firefox 155 with Playwright 1.63.0 and is accurate: the chapter's first program printed the title, goto() returned the Response whose repr the chapter shows, the six locators of Table 13-5 each found their element on the example page, locators are strict (inner_text() on a locator of seven paragraphs raised a strict mode violation, which is why the chapter picks one with nth(0)), a click on a mistyped selector waited 30 seconds and raised TimeoutError while is_visible() and query_selector() answered at once, click() toggled a checkbox where check(), uncheck() and set_checked() set it, a Locator has no submit method, End and Home scrolled the page, Control+A selected the text of the page, and a headed launch without a display failed with a message about the missing XServer; the browsers are installed by a second step, playwright install, whose dry run lists Chrome for Testing, Chrome Headless Shell, Firefox, WebKit and FFmpeg." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P23, chx:P24, chx:P25, chx:P26 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "What a program may fetch and with what identity is a question the chapter touches only through bandwidth, through the advice to prefer an API and through the secrecy of the key: the Robots Exclusion Protocol of RFC 9309 states that its rules are not a form of access authorization, so a robots.txt is a request that a polite crawler honours and the standard library can read with urllib.robotparser (which exposed Crawl-delay and Request-rate, and a crawler that read them waited between its pages); an API key identifies an account, the fake key of the chapter's example was answered by OpenWeather with status 401 and a JSON message on 2026-10-12, the free plan allows 60 calls a minute, and the weather service api.weather.gov, which the chapter names, answered with forecast periods and no key but documents that a User-Agent identifying the application is required." ; sen0414:cites chx:P28, chx:P05, chx:P01, chx:P35, chx:P36, chx:P37 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "A page built by a script shows why the chapter keeps a second family of tools: on a local page whose only paragraph is written by JavaScript, requests returned the script and an empty element, Beautiful Soup found the element with no text, and Selenium and Playwright in Firefox returned the written text; the chapter's advice to prefer a site's API holds on the day, since the NWS API returned fourteen forecast periods in JSON for the chapter's latitude and longitude while the forecast page of weather.gov that the chapter inspects still carried fourteen elements of the class forecast-text." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P37, chx:P17, chx:P23 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's three practice programs were written and run against local stand-ins: link verification resolved relative links against the page URL, dropped the fragment so that a page linked twice counted once, skipped other sites and printed the one link that answered 404; an image site downloader saved the images of a results page with iter_content; and a crawler that read robots.txt first obeyed Crawl-delay and skipped a disallowed path; the game 2048 was not run, because it needs a browser on the live https site (the site answered 200 on 2026-10-12)." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P05, chx:P15, chx:P17, chx:P28 .
chx:F12 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 13 is best taught as a choice of tool by the page in front of the program: webbrowser when the user should see the page, requests with raise_for_status and a timeout when the URL is known and the answer is text or a file, an API when the service offers one, Beautiful Soup on the text received when the page is static, and Selenium or Playwright only when the page needs a script or a login; the chapter's offline-checkable outputs hold, ten printed items do not run as printed, searchpypi.py meets a challenge page and a robots rule that changed meaning in Python 3.14.5, and what an advanced course adds is timeouts, streaming, robots.txt and its Python history, the newer Selenium and Playwright behaviour, the flags a browser reveals and the deprecations of current Python." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P15, chx:P17, chx:P18, chx:P23, chx:P28, chx:P14 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "47 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "42 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-12T14:00:00; 30 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
